    "finalPromptText": "One last thing — for my bibliography and literature review, I'd like to know what other major works Tufte published after The Visual Display of Quantitative Information. Did he write follow-up books on similar topics? I want to situate his 1983 book within his broader body of work on information design.",
    "finalAssistantVisibleText": "Edward R. Tufte published several key works following *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information*, which further explore themes of data visualization and information design. Here are his major subsequent publications:\n\n1. **Envisioning Information (1990)**  \n   This work builds upon the principles established in his first book, focusing on how to present complex information clearly and effectively. Tufte explores various graphic designs and emphasizes the importance of integrating qualitative data with quantitative.\n\n2. **Visual Explanations (1997)**  \n   This book delves into the relationship between data and explanation. Tufte presents case studies from diverse fields and discusses how effective visualization can enhance comprehension and convey complex concepts more clearly.\n\n3. **Beautiful Evidence (2006)**  \n   Tufte's latest influential work focuses on the aesthetics of information display and the role of visual evidence in understanding data. He emphasizes the merging of beauty with clarity, arguing that good design can convey truths more effectively.\n\nThese works collectively situate *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information* within a broader narrative about the evolution of data visualization and information design across Tufte's career.\n\nFor more detailed information on all of Tufte's works, you can visit the complete bibliography [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/edward-tufte/works) or check his biography [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/edward-tufte). These sources should be helpful in constructing your literature review and establishing Tufte's contributions to the field.",
    "finalAssistantRawText": "Edward R. Tufte published several key works following *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information*, which further explore themes of data visualization and information design. Here are his major subsequent publications:\n\n1. **Envisioning Information (1990)**  \n   This work builds upon the principles established in his first book, focusing on how to present complex information clearly and effectively. Tufte explores various graphic designs and emphasizes the importance of integrating qualitative data with quantitative.\n\n2. **Visual Explanations (1997)**  \n   This book delves into the relationship between data and explanation. Tufte presents case studies from diverse fields and discusses how effective visualization can enhance comprehension and convey complex concepts more clearly.\n\n3. **Beautiful Evidence (2006)**  \n   Tufte's latest influential work focuses on the aesthetics of information display and the role of visual evidence in understanding data. He emphasizes the merging of beauty with clarity, arguing that good design can convey truths more effectively.\n\nThese works collectively situate *The Visual Display of Quantitative Information* within a broader narrative about the evolution of data visualization and information design across Tufte's career.\n\nFor more detailed information on all of Tufte's works, you can visit the complete bibliography [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/edward-tufte/works) or check his biography [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/edward-tufte). These sources should be helpful in constructing your literature review and establishing Tufte's contributions to the field.",
